  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n - Warming and drying trend through the weekend.\n\n - Thunderstorms return to southwest Montana on Monday, the rest\n   of western Montana on Tuesday. \n\n - An upper low arrives Monday night into Tuesday causing a cool\n   down and active, wet weather the rest of the week. \n\nA warming and drying trend under a broad, weak ridge will last\nthrough the weekend. Temperatures will rise to about 15 degrees\nabove average by Monday. \n\nEnsembles agree very well on a closed upper level low moving into\nthe region late Monday into Tuesday. This will cause a drop in\ntemperatures to about 5 degrees below average for this time of\nyear. The most uncertainty remains in the precipitation amounts\nthat the region will receive. On the low end (10th percentile) the\nmountains of west-central Montana pick up around a quarter of an\ninch with northwest Montana being dry and generally a few\nhundredths to a tenth of an inch elsewhere. On the high end (90th\npercentile) areas along the Continental Divide in northwest\nMontana pick up over 1.5 inches of rain over 48 hours. Exactly how\nit plays out will depend on subtle differences in the track of the\nlow as it crosses the region. \n\nAfter the sharp cool down on Tuesday, temperatures will trend\nwarmer the rest of next week with active weather as the upper\nlevel low stumbles out of the region.",
